{"review_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0", "passages": [{"quote": "I like the insight that method can use the model's predictions and gradients and thus we should be aware of model gradient errors.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "insight quality"}, {"quote": "The experimental results in Figure 1 show the sample efficiency and show an improvement over the relevant baselines, using 5 trials.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "sample efficiency improvement"}, {"quote": "I especially like the ablation in Figure 3(right)/Figure 4 that ablates against the model not using the separate gradients.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "ablation study quality"}, {"quote": "some of the empirical gain may come from modifying the base model-based algorithm rather than adding the gradient information.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "attribution of improvement"}, {"quote": "This seems potentially intractable in practice for large replay buffers", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "scalability"}, {"quote": "the end of Section 5.1 says that the results of ~5k reward attain \"near-optimal policies in the Humanoid environment\" (for running) despite papers such as the [SAC-SVG] attaining a reward of ~9-10k", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "misleading results claims"}, {"quote": "I think this is extremely misleading and should not be accepted for publication.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "misleading presentation"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0", "passages": [{"quote": "its algorithm construction is well-reasoned", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "algorithm design quality"}, {"quote": "the theory appears to be novel and provides appropriate support for the proposed algorithm", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "theoretical novelty and support"}, {"quote": "the empirical results provide some minor support for the claims", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "empirical support strength"}, {"quote": "I find that this paper contributes an important perspective on the role of using differentiable models", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "conceptual contribution"}, {"quote": "the empirical results provide only marginal support for the proposed algorithm", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "empirical support strength"}, {"quote": "there is a disconnect between the paper's stated goals and the novelty the paper _actually_ provides", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "stated vs actual contribution"}, {"quote": "the paper does not provide sufficient information about the baselines to understand if this experiment is fair", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental fairness clarity"}, {"quote": "The use of only 5 random seeds is also concerning, especially considering the very high variance exhibited in many of the reported results.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "statistical rigor"}, {"quote": "it seems very challenging to support the claim that the proposed algorithm achieves state-of-the-art results", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "SOTA claim support"}, {"quote": "the real value provided by this paper is further insight into the need to control the gradient error when using differentiable models", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "insight into gradient error"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0", "passages": [{"quote": "the core idea the authors to improve model-based reinforcement learning are well motivated", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "motivation quality"}, {"quote": "The proposed solution is interesting and novel.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "the numerical results are impressive", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "numerical results quality"}, {"quote": "I don't see enough explanation or intuitions for them.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "explanation and intuition"}, {"quote": "the experiments need to be presented with more understanding for the results to be reproduced", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility"}, {"quote": "the writing of this paper could be substantially improved. The writing has many typos and the structure is not very clear.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality and clarity"}, {"quote": "In a couple reads I never understood the part on the directional derivative as a scalar.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "clarity of explanation"}, {"quote": "This section was a bit too broad, which made it hard to get the point of the paper across.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "focus and clarity"}, {"quote": "the formatting here is really forced and deteriorates the reading experience.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "formatting quality"}, {"quote": "The related works seem dismissive of the past work.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "treatment of prior work"}, {"quote": "This section needs substantially more explanation to support the math.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "mathematical explanation"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_rzvOQrnclO0", "passages": [{"quote": "The method presented is quite original and well-positioned among existing methods for model learning.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "originality and positioning"}, {"quote": "The problem in object is of interest for the RL community.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem significance"}, {"quote": "The theoretical results are not of particular benefit for the practical algorithm.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theory-practice connection"}, {"quote": "the core change proposed by the method does not provide very large improvements over baselines", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "improvement magnitude"}, {"quote": "Directly learning the Jacobians of the dynamics is a very appealing goal", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "goal appeal"}, {"quote": "The presented approach is well-positioned in the literature as a *decision-aware* approach for model learning.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "positioning in literature"}, {"quote": "The theoretical results presented in Section 4, despite not being novel in essence, are quite interesting.", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "theoretical novelty and interest"}, {"quote": "Both the proposed loss function for model learning and the idea of having two models for trajectory generation and gradient computation are, as far as I know, new and simple to implement.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty and simplicity"}, {"quote": "The loss function in Equation 12 requires to select the nearest neighbours of the current data point. With the usual implementation for the replay buffer, this adds a significant computational burden", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "computational complexity"}, {"quote": "there is the risk that much of the improvement provided by the use of two models actually comes from the additional capacity", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "proper attribution of gains"}, {"quote": "The mechanism used for backpropagating through a model after generating a trajectory from another one is unclear.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "clarity of mechanism"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ", "passages": [{"quote": "The framework is new, and the results are promising.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty and results"}, {"quote": "the scientific depth seems a bit shallow", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "scientific depth"}, {"quote": "Since ICLR is a top machine learning conference, I would suggest the authors further highlight the major contributions, especially the scientific novelty", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "contribution clarity for venue"}, {"quote": "Additional experiments should be conducted to confirm the effectiveness of the proposed approach.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental completeness"}, {"quote": "The paper is well-written, and the theoretical part should be correct.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing and correctness"}, {"quote": "The promising results confirm the effectiveness of the proposed SSA to attack the ASR system.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "effectiveness of method"}, {"quote": "The authors also provide a comprehensive analysis of hyper-parameters setups to the achievable performance.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "hyperparameter analysis thoroughness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ", "passages": [{"quote": "The most interesting component of this paper is the fact that adversarial attacks can be constructed by manipulation of the prosodic characteristics of synthetic speech.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "interesting attack mechanism"}, {"quote": "it is not at all clear that the use of synthesis and prosodic manipulation is a more compelling attack than adding adversarial noise to carrier speech", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "attack advantage clarity"}, {"quote": "The context where there is no available carrier speech is unconvincing", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "scenario motivation"}, {"quote": "The white-box assumption is reasonable for this kind of proof of concept, but it's less convincing than a black-box attack.", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "attack assumption limitation"}, {"quote": "There is no assessment of TTS quality either of the un-modified initial utterance (that should be recognized correctly by the target ASR model) or the adversarial utterance.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "TTS quality evaluation"}, {"quote": "the naturalness is substantially impaired (i.e. to my ear many are obviously synthetic speech)", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "naturalness of synthesis"}, {"quote": "This is a limitation especially in comparison to audio dependent attacks (ADA) which sound like natural speech with a small amount of background noise.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparison to baseline quality"}, {"quote": "the claim that this objective function \"guarantees [that] the audio x [is] natural sounding\" is over stated.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "overclaimed guarantees"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper does provide a new adversarial attack mode that is different from the audio \"dependent\" attack (ADA) methods.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty of attack mode"}, {"quote": "the synthesized speech signals are indeed in good quality, i.e., they do sound natural and convey the original meaning", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "synthesis quality"}, {"quote": "The targeted attack results are substantially better than the other models.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "targeted attack performance"}, {"quote": "It is not too clear to me what is the real-world attack scenario based on the proposed method.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "real-world scenario clarity"}, {"quote": "The paper does not discuss this point of view, although it must nicely justify why the synthesis-based attacks are useful.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "justification of usefulness"}, {"quote": "the proposed model is lacking a discussion as to how the attacker can successfully use the synthesized voice", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "attack scenario discussion"}, {"quote": "I believe that a large-scale evaluation on (a) sound quality of the synthesized speech (b) human annotator's transcription is necessary.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "evaluation completeness"}, {"quote": "it is still not too clear if it is the best way", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "loss function justification"}, {"quote": "Additional discussion on the black-box attack cases my strengthen the paper.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "black-box attack discussion"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_bE239PSGIGZ", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper is well written, clear, the problem stated concisely, ample references are provided, and the experiments are convincing.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing and experimental quality"}, {"quote": "This novel type of attack - synthesizing audio instead of perturbing - is interesting", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty of approach"}, {"quote": "the authors present their method clearly", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "clarity of presentation"}, {"quote": "The quantitative presentation is good and the inclusion of their adaptive sign gradient decent algorithm may be useful for further researchers.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "quantitative presentation quality"}, {"quote": "The authors show the validity of their SSA approach by demonstrating its superior ability to attack an ASR system.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "validity demonstration"}, {"quote": "this is maybe not so surprising given the fast that their SSA approach is able to modify the input in many ways, whereas the perturbation approaches are limited", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "expected nature of results"}, {"quote": "The only minor point is that there was no MOS done on samples the authors generated.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "MOS evaluation missing"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09", "passages": [{"quote": "While the idea is interesting, my main concern is about the novelty and the appropriate reference of previous work.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty and prior work credit"}, {"quote": "the first-order necessary stationary condition was already used together with REINFORCE in previous work (see [31])", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of technical approach"}, {"quote": "I think a deeper discussion of the differences between your and their work is necessary.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparison to prior work"}, {"quote": "I think it is also necessary to add their algorithms to the empirical comparison.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline completeness"}, {"quote": "I would have appreciated a more extended comparison. For example, why do you focus on domains with very few objectives", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental scope"}, {"quote": "there are a few typos in the paper that makes the reading complicated, especially in section 3", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09", "passages": [{"quote": "This work is written well and easy to follow.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing clarity"}, {"quote": "I didn't find major drawbacks in the paper", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "overall soundness"}, {"quote": "the theoretical results seem correct (yet not very novel or fundamental)", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "theoretical novelty"}, {"quote": "Overall the work seems solid to me.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "overall solidity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_wfZGut6e09", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper is well written and the method is well presented.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing and presentation"}, {"quote": "The introduction covers well prior work.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "I think that the authors do not properly compare their method to the existing ones discussed in the introduction, and I find it hard to distinguish between what is really novel and what has already been done.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty clarity"}, {"quote": "everything up to line 150 is already known (correct me if I am wrong)", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of CCS section"}, {"quote": "The first-order condition is a necessary but not sufficient condition for Pareto optimality. You discuss this but only at the very end", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "limitation discussion placement"}, {"quote": "you should mention that the Deep Sea Treasure version used for the experiments is not the original version", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental setup disclosure"}, {"quote": "the adaptation part is highly related to Envelope Q-Learning, which you use as baseline in the experiments. You should discuss similarities and differences more in-depth", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparison to related method"}, {"quote": "It is a bit misleading to claim in the abstract that the final PPA method learns under "unknown preferences" since the objective in (8) does require $\\omega$ to evaluate the loss.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "misleading claim"}, {"quote": "It would be helpful to compare PPA with other stronger baselines, such as PGMORL", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline strength"}, {"quote": "You should discuss PPA limitation more in details.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "limitation discussion depth"}, {"quote": "Why did you not use the hypervolume as evaluation criterion? It is probably the most common and accurate criterion in MORL literature", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "metric choice justification"}, {"quote": "I am not convinced by your intuition that \"given the complex inter-dependence between the two rewards, simply hand-crafting a preference vector is far from optimal\"", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "intuition justification"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M", "passages": [{"quote": "Considering synthesized OOD sample to expose model is more feasible compared to procuring real OOD samples.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approach feasibility"}, {"quote": "Authors synthesis the OOD samples in the low-dimensional feature space which is more efficient to generate than the actual image space.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "efficiency of synthesis"}, {"quote": "Unknown-aware training is also an effective idea to differentiate the in distribution samples from OOD samples.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "training approach effectiveness"}, {"quote": "Experiments are conducted on the benchmark dataset and results are impressive. Extensive experiments and ablation studies are conducted", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental thoroughness"}, {"quote": "the novelty of the proposed approach is limited. Considering synthesized outliers to detect OOD or novel objects are explored in various previous approaches.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "the class boundaries in the high-dimensional space may not be Gaussian", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "distributional assumption validity"}, {"quote": "the object detector may miss a significant number of bounding boxes as some of the objects instances may not appear in the in-distribution data", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "object detection coverage"}, {"quote": "What is the motivation for considering the synthetic OOD samples when real outlier data are abundant, easily available, and achieve better performance.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "motivation vs alternatives"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M", "passages": [{"quote": "Sampling artificial training samples in the feature space appears effective.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approach effectiveness"}, {"quote": "There is some novelty in the formulations of uncertainty score (learned weights) and outlier probability", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty in formulation"}, {"quote": "The authors took time to implement and evaluate several prominent baselines. Experimental evaluation shows competitive performance.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "baseline evaluation thoroughness"}, {"quote": "Reading the paper requires a lot of guesswork.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "clarity"}, {"quote": "Figure 2 suggests that the outliers are detected within RPN while in fact the paper considers only ROI classifier", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "figure clarity"}, {"quote": "The paper does not discuss the computational complexity of recovering \\Sigma.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "computational complexity discussion"}, {"quote": "The following work should be cited and discussed since it considers logit normalization for outlier detection", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "The following work should be cited and discussed since it considers learning open-set recognition with artificial outliers", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work coverage"}]}
{"review_id": "ANCH-1", "paper_id": "ANCH-1", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper tackles an interesting problem.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem interest"}, {"quote": "the novelty is limited: the proposed method is a straightforward combination of two existing techniques", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "The writing is also difficult to follow in Sections 3-4, with undefined notation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing clarity"}, {"quote": "the experimental evaluation is thorough, covering five datasets with proper ablations", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental thoroughness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M", "passages": [{"quote": "This paper is well written and easy to understand. Especially, the figures deliver the core idea of the whole paper.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing clarity and figures"}, {"quote": "The proposed method is straightforward but efficient. Also, the idea could be proved by a toy example.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "method simplicity and efficiency"}, {"quote": "Some extreme cases are not clearly illustrated. For example, the sampling process is done online. In one batch, it is likely to have only one instance in some class.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "extreme case handling"}, {"quote": "I am very concerned about the quality of estimation.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "estimation quality"}, {"quote": "Too many ablations studies are arranged on supplementary files.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation study placement"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_TW7d65uYu5M", "passages": [{"quote": "This work looks solid, and the experiments in this paper are sufficient, which consider almost all key parameters or components of the proposed method and demonstrate the effectiveness", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental sufficiency"}, {"quote": "the method achieves state-of-the-art performance on both object detection and image classification tasks", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "SOTA performance"}, {"quote": "This paper is well motivated, which aims to utilize synthetic virtual outliers to regularize the model, thus avoiding the collection and cleaning of real OOD data.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "motivation quality"}, {"quote": "A few details in the proposed method are not very clear. Specifically, what are the motivation and mechanism of the proposed generalized energy score?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "clarity of details"}, {"quote": "How to train the learnable weight coefficients? How to update the ID queue? It would be better to clarify these issues.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "clarity of mechanisms"}, {"quote": "Since the real outlier data may not be tightly distributed around the ID data in raw or feature space, why the performance of OE can be viewed as a performance upper bound of VOS?", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "assumption justification"}, {"quote": "I wonder what virtual outliers look like. It might be better to visualize them.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "visualization of outputs"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w", "passages": [{"quote": "Contrary to previous work, the proposed method does not pre- or post-processing to make sure the returned graph is DAG.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "DAG guarantee method"}, {"quote": "It is easy to implement the algorithm in a few lines of code.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "implementation simplicity"}, {"quote": "The current work presents a differentiable DAG sampling which I think is novel in this field.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty of DAG sampling"}, {"quote": "From the experimental results, it is hard to conclude that the current work is better than previous works in terms of both processing time and estimation error.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "comparative performance clarity"}, {"quote": "It would be great if the authors can derive the computational complexities of other previous works and compare them with their method.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "computational complexity comparison"}, {"quote": "From table 9, we can see that the performances of all methods get better as we use non-differentiable processing and even other methods (like CAM) might achieve higher performance.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "relative performance"}, {"quote": "It is unclear whether the DAG sampler is unbiased, i.e., it selects a DAG uniformly from the Markov equivalence class as number of samples goes to infinity.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "sampler bias property"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w", "passages": [{"quote": "The method is simple and clever. Rather than relying on relaxations during the forward pass, the Gumbel Straight-Through estimator allows the relaxation to happen only during gradient estimation.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "method cleverness"}, {"quote": "The experimental evaluation is thorough. The model is evaluated on both synthetic (Erdos-Renyi, Scale-Free, and SynTReN) and real data (Sachs) of various sizes. The results are convincing", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental thoroughness"}, {"quote": "VI-DP-DAG either achieving the best performance or comparable for structure learning. A similar trend holds for causal mechanism learning", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "comparative performance"}, {"quote": "Timing results are very impressive. VI-DP-DAG obtains 5x-10x speedup over baselines. In conjunction with the accuracy results, this is a strong contribution.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "timing performance"}, {"quote": "The paper is clearly written.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing clarity"}, {"quote": "I did not find any obvious weaknesses.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "overall quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_9wOQOgNe-w", "passages": [{"quote": "this paper focuses on the problem of DAG structure learning using gradient-based methods", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem focus"}, {"quote": "the proposed method of this paper is only a combination of well-developed techniques", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty"}, {"quote": "The start point of this paper is Theorem 1, which states that any adjacency matrix of a DAG can be obtained by simultaneously applying row and column-wise permutation to an upper triangular matrix. This result is trivial and has been known for a very long time.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theoretical novelty"}, {"quote": "I think it is not appropriate to state it as a theorem.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theorem appropriateness"}, {"quote": "Theorem 1 has been applied to sampling DAGs already. In fact, many packages use this result to generate random DAGs.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of application"}, {"quote": "to make the sampling procedure differentiable, this paper directly uses the recently developed Gumbel-Softmax, Gumbel-Sinkhorn, and Gumbel-Top-k tricks", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of technique"}, {"quote": "the idea of separately sampling an upper triangular matrix and a permutation matrix in order to sample a DAG is not novel", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "idea novelty"}, {"quote": "the experiments need to include more metrics", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "metric completeness"}, {"quote": "Overall, this paper is well-written.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing quality"}, {"quote": "the contributions are only marginally significant or novel", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "contribution significance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5", "passages": [{"quote": "The overall performance seems to be better than the state-of-the-art.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "overall performance"}, {"quote": "The motivation of each component is intuitive and straightforward.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "component motivation clarity"}, {"quote": "The presentation of this paper is clear.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "presentation clarity"}, {"quote": "The baselines compared are from 2016 & 2018. How're latest competitions or related work after them? It seems like this part of the related work is missing", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline recency"}, {"quote": "The ablation study in this paper is not convincing enough to show the usefulness of different components and different stages.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation study convincingness"}, {"quote": "there is no clear evidence showing that the whole process has to be done in a three-stage way", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "staging justification"}, {"quote": "The source code of this paper is unavailable, which makes it hard to reproduce and understand the details in the training process.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "code availability"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5", "passages": [{"quote": "The paper is well-written and the logic is clear.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing clarity"}, {"quote": "The visualization in the experiment clearly illustrates that the agent learns how to navigate, shoot, and other strategies.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "visualization quality"}, {"quote": "The paper provides a careful design for the FPS game and achieves superior performance.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "design quality and performance"}, {"quote": "there are no more techniques to reduce the high variance brought by cumulative multiplication terms", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "variance reduction"}, {"quote": "the paper does not compare the HPPO with other goal-conditioned approaches in the experiment", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline comparison completeness"}, {"quote": "I'm wondering why choosing different regions can represent different strategy styles.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "strategy representation clarity"}, {"quote": "I'm wondering how the agent cumulates Frag scores in unseen areas when testing, and whether the auxiliary network's prediction is accurate when facing unknown opponents.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "generalization clarity"}, {"quote": "The experimental setup in evaluating strategy diversity is unfair. The evaluation opponent(Goal-00 to Goal-19) is used when training the DSC-Agent in stage 3.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental fairness"}, {"quote": "The paper should conduct more experiments to evaluate the strategic diversity of the agent which is the main contribution of the paper.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental coverage of main claim"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5", "passages": [{"quote": "This work takes a problem-driven approach to improve the performance of a reinforcement learning agent on VizDoom.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem-driven approach"}, {"quote": "I consider the present work to be valuable as it assembles and confronts some well-known techniques in RL to concrete problems.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "value of assembling techniques"}, {"quote": "The impact of the action wrapper and the rule-guided policy search is a great example that sometimes less is more.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "simplicity effectiveness"}, {"quote": "The quality and clarity of the writing is problematic. Besides the numerous spelling mistakes, the imprecise language often confuses the reader.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality and clarity"}, {"quote": "the proposed approach consist of a combination of existing techniques. Although replicating and using these techniques require significant engineering effort, these can't be considered novel contributions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of contributions"}, {"quote": "The work is certainly complex from an engineering point of view: handcrafted curriculum learning, hindsight experience replay, distributed training strategy. As such, particular attention to providing all the required information to replicate the work has to be initiated. This aspect of the work is deeply lacking.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility information"}, {"quote": "The values of all the hyper-parameters are not provided e.g. optimizers, losses coefficients, training parameters, etc.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "hyperparameter reporting"}, {"quote": "Many terms and aspects are not defined. The authors don't explain: how they applied Policy Distillation, how the prioritized self-play has been implemented and put in action", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "method detail clarity"}, {"quote": "These missing bits of information would make the replication of the results nearly impossible to achieve and don't help the reader understand how the approach fully works", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "replicability"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_tvwNdOKhuF5", "passages": [{"quote": "Experiments are well-designed, and ablation studies are also presented.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "experimental design"}, {"quote": "Visualizations make the paper much more interesting and understandable.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "visualization quality"}, {"quote": "This paper shows that instead of using larger NN, assembling existing techniques are also able to achieve SOTA performance.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approach effectiveness"}, {"quote": "Although the network is trained end-to-end, they do use some hand-crafted domain specific rules in stage 2 and stage 3.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "hand-crafting reliance"}, {"quote": "in this multi-stage learning system, each stage is manually selected/formed", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "manual stage design"}, {"quote": "The neural network architecture is provided, but the used code and settings of hyper-parameters are not presented.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "code and hyperparameter availability"}, {"quote": "1152 CPUs and 32 GPUs are used, so I guess this work could be really hard to reproduce.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility difficulty"}, {"quote": "Only a single game is used for empirical validation, the proposed method is quite complex, consisting of the combination of a number of existing methods", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "empirical scope"}, {"quote": "the clarity of the contributed method is not sufficient, and no explanation is provided to explain how the method works and why the performance is better", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "method explanation clarity"}, {"quote": "Overall the work is somewhat interesting, but not significant enough to warrant publication.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "publication significance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a", "passages": [{"quote": "The research about channel pruning is insufficient, with only six related works, and the author should summarize the differences with them.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "Hierarchical distillation of knowledge seems to be a traditional approach, which should not be a contribution to this work.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "novelty of contribution"}, {"quote": "the authors should verify the effectiveness of the proposed method by comparing it with various channel pruning and knowledge distillation methods", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline comparison breadth"}, {"quote": "The author should analyze the differences between the two parts, eg. New pruning algorithm and Hierarchical knowledge distillation, with other methods in the ablation study.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation study depth"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a", "passages": [{"quote": "This paper aims at solving the coupled channels in channel pruning, which is important in practice and needs more investigations.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem importance"}, {"quote": "The evaluated tasks (image denoising and human segmentation) seem to be quite interesting and seldomly discussed in related pruning works.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "task selection novelty"}, {"quote": "It is good to observe and conclude that KD only works for pruned networks but not for unpruned (original) networks. However, more investigations are welcomed to make confident claims and hypotheses.", "polarity": "mixed", "aspect": "observation value"}, {"quote": "There are only a few baseline pruning methods compared. Meanwhile, results on ResNet should be compared. Otherwise, it is difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "baseline comparison breadth"}, {"quote": "The tables show merely the same results as those in the figures (e.g., Table 1 & Figure 4, Tables 2/3 & Figure 5.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "result presentation redundancy"}, {"quote": "No ablation studies. It seems that the most performance gain is from the hierarchical KD, which is not exclusive to other pruning methods.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "ablation study absence"}, {"quote": "Applying hierarchical KD seems not to have enough methodological novelty or contributions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "methodological novelty"}, {"quote": "The writing clarity should be moderately addressed. For example, the descriptions of the two phases in Section 4.1 are quite informal and vague", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing clarity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_Ab0o8YMJ8a", "passages": [{"quote": "The proposed pruning scheme solves the dimension mismatching problem, when pruning some specific model architecture (such as ResNet).", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "dimension matching solution"}, {"quote": "The hierarchical KD can promote the performance, which avoids the problem: "too large teacher does not always make better student".", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "hierarchical KD effectiveness"}, {"quote": "Although each part of the proposed method is effective, the overall algorithm is still cumbersome. It has multiple stages. In contrast, many of existing pruning methods do not need fine-tuning.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "algorithm complexity"}, {"quote": "Technical details and formulations are limited. It seems that the main novelty reflected in the scheme or procedure novelty.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical novelty"}, {"quote": "The experimental results are not convincing. The compared methods are few.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "experimental comparison scope"}, {"quote": "the performance gains compared with SOTAs are also marginal, which are also within 1%", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "improvement magnitude"}, {"quote": "The paper is poorly written. There are many typos", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "writing quality"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n", "passages": [{"quote": "this paper provides some interesting ideas on how to study the universal lottery ticket hypothesis", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "interesting ideas"}, {"quote": "the results are not rigorous enough and the sparsity level of the lottery ticket is not addressed properly", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "rigor and sparsity treatment"}, {"quote": "it is still not very clear to me what exactly is the sparsity level of the universal lottery ticket that is proven to exist. This is very important", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "sparsity clarity"}, {"quote": "I feel that this may not be sparse enough. When the sparsity is just at a constant level, it seems that we can simply randomly delete a constant proportion of the neurons in each layer", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "sparsity sufficiency"}, {"quote": "Theorem 1 is also a bit confusing.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theorem clarity"}, {"quote": "The result of this paper is also a bit incremental, as it is mainly a combination of known results on using polynomial/Fourier bases to approximate smooth functions and using neural networks to approximate polynomial/Fourier bases.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "incremental nature of result"}, {"quote": "the authors may consider presenting formal theorems in the main paper, and add discussions to justify the results", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "theorem presentation"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n_r1", "paper_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n", "passages": [{"quote": "this paper provides some novel results and intuitive ideas to show the existence of universal lottery tickets", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "novelty and intuitiveness"}, {"quote": "shows that depth can be used to reduce the width required for strong lottery ticket hypothesis", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "depth-width tradeoff"}, {"quote": "the parameter $N$ in Theorem 1 might be of the order $dk/\\epsilon$. In that case, the required width $n_{l,0}$ becomes super linear in $d$ and $k$, and no-longer remains logarithmic in $\\epsilon$. This would be bad", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "width scaling concern"}, {"quote": "The paper only talks about approximating the functions in the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, whereas the more interesting case is approximating classes of functions such as Lipschitz-continuous functions.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "approximation scope limitation"}, {"quote": "applying this paper's results directly for that setting would lead to the classic 'curse of dimensionality'", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "curse of dimensionality"}, {"quote": "I think the main paper (first 9 pages) is not able to explain a lot of important aspects and I had to go through the appendix to understand them.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "main paper completeness"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n_r2", "paper_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n", "passages": [{"quote": "Overall this paper is technically sound and well organized.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "technical soundness and organization"}, {"quote": "the major contribution compared to existing works (in terms of the theoretical results) is that this paper does not require the mother network to have depth $2L$ to approximate the target network with depth $L$, but could be of any depth", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "depth flexibility contribution"}, {"quote": "theorem 1 states that the universal approximation is taken with respect to the function family $F(B)$. But as claimed by the authors, functions in $F(B)$ are not exactly the target neural network function $f$. There seemly has a gap", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "approximation target gap"}, {"quote": "I do not quite get the illustration in Figure 1. It seems that the authors want to show how to develop an architecture to approximate a monomial. However, as stated on page 4, g(x) and h(x) are exponential and logarithmic functions", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "figure clarity"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n_r3", "paper_id": "iclr_SYB4WrJql1n", "passages": [{"quote": "I think the paper is well written and studies an interesting direction.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "writing and direction"}, {"quote": "the number of neurons is linearly proportional to the number of base functions |B|. Such linear seems to make the result a bit trivial than expected.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "linear dependency triviality"}, {"quote": "it seems this paper utilizes the above way for construction with additional effort to deal with the scaling issue, which is not difficult", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "technical difficulty"}, {"quote": "due to some concerns on the significance of the result, I am not sure whether the result is above the acceptance bar of ICLR", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "result significance"}]}
{"review_id": "ANCH-2", "paper_id": "ANCH-2", "passages": [{"quote": "This submission provides a rigorous theoretical analysis; the proofs appear correct and the assumptions are clearly stated.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "theoretical rigor"}, {"quote": "My main concern is reproducibility: no code is provided and several hyperparameters are unreported.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "reproducibility"}, {"quote": "The related-work coverage is adequate.", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "related work coverage"}, {"quote": "Overall the significance for the broader community seems modest.", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "broader significance"}]}
{"review_id": "iclr_hZftxQGJ4Re_r0", "paper_id": "iclr_hZftxQGJ4Re", "passages": [{"quote": "the authors propose Deep Graph Ensemble (DGE) to tackle the additional information available in higher order networks $-$ something which standard Message Passing GNNs methods are unable to process without loss of information", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "problem addressed"}, {"quote": "The authors propose to do this by leveraging an ensemble of standard MPNNs - which are used to solve the 3 design challenges associated with HONs", "polarity": "pos", "aspect": "approach to design challenges"}, {"quote": "in the current state , the weaknesses of the paper out weigh the strengths", "polarity": "neg", "aspect": "overall balance"}]}
